1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,534
Answering Slack messages in thirty seconds

2
00:00:02,534 --> 00:00:03,560
feels productive,

3
00:00:03,560 --> 00:00:05,974
yet computer scientist Cal Newport warns

4
00:00:05,974 --> 00:00:07,905
this habit of constant motion is

5
00:00:07,905 --> 00:00:10,560
silently destroying your best career output.

6
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:12,312
In his book Slow Productivity,

7
00:00:12,312 --> 00:00:16,108
Newport diagnoses modern knowledge work with pseudo-productivity:

8
00:00:16,108 --> 00:00:17,451
using visible busyness,

9
00:00:17,451 --> 00:00:18,443
packed calendars,

10
00:00:18,443 --> 00:00:20,370
and instant replies as a stand-in

11
00:00:20,370 --> 00:00:21,480
for useful results.

12
00:00:21,480 --> 00:00:23,542
Every time you juggle seven active

13
00:00:23,542 --> 00:00:25,543
projects and thirty chat threads,

14
00:00:25,543 --> 00:00:28,454
context-switching taxes your brain until nothing

15
00:00:28,454 --> 00:00:30,880
meaningful ever crosses the finish line.

16
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:32,986
Newport's cure has three rules:

17
00:00:32,986 --> 00:00:35,228
cap major active projects at two,

18
00:00:35,228 --> 00:00:37,674
work at a sustainable seasonal pace,

19
00:00:37,674 --> 00:00:40,460
and obsess over undeniable craft quality.

20
00:00:40,460 --> 00:00:41,997
Five years from now,

21
00:00:41,997 --> 00:00:44,763
nobody will remember how quickly you

22
00:00:44,763 --> 00:00:47,068
cleared your inbox - they will

23
00:00:47,068 --> 00:00:49,911
only remember the flagship assets you

24
00:00:49,911 --> 00:00:51,294
actually finished.

25
00:00:51,294 --> 00:00:52,370
That is why...
